    "note": "In everyday speech the most common way to say this is the phrase **on purpose**: *You did that on purpose!* **Deliberately** is common in speech and writing and is often used for something bad or unkind: *He deliberately lied to us*. **Intentionally** is more formal: *The rule was intentionally left out*."
  },
  "usage_note": "**Deliberately** usually comes before the main verb (*She deliberately left early*), before an adjective (*deliberately simple*), or at the end of the clause (*The fire was started deliberately*).",
  "learner_errors": [
    {
      "incorrect": "He broke it by purpose.",
      "correct": "He broke it deliberately.",
      "note": "Say **deliberately** or **on purpose**, not **by purpose**."
    },
    {
      "incorrect": "She deliberate ignored me.",
      "correct": "She deliberately ignored me.",
      "note": "Before a verb, use the adverb **deliberately**, not the adjective **deliberate**."
    }
  ],
